    {
      "id": "w4-l3",
      "week": 4,
      "order": 3,
      "title": "Amharic, nine, Monday",
      "goal": "Add ኘ and ዠ, and hear doubled sounds.",
      "newFamilies": [
        "ኘ",
        "ዠ"
      ],
      "estimatedMinutes": 20,
      "cultureNote": "አማርኛ is the working language of Ethiopia's federal government. The fidel you're learning is also used to write Tigrinya and other languages of Ethiopia and Eritrea.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "type": "review"
        },
        {
          "type": "hear",
          "items": [
            "ኘ",
            "ኙ",
            "ኚ",
            "ኛ",
            "ኜ",
            "ኝ",
            "ኞ",
            "ዠ",
            "ዡ",
            "ዢ",
            "ዣ",
            "ዤ",
            "ዥ",
            "ዦ"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "see",
          "family": "ኘ"
        },
        {
          "type": "see",
          "family": "ዠ"
        },
        {
          "type": "rule",
          "title": "ኘ says ny",
          "text": "ኘ is ny, as in 'canyon' or Spanish 'señor'. It's ነ with an extra mark on top, like the letters yesterday. ዠ is zh, like the s in 'measure' — it comes from ዘ, and it's rare."
        },
        {
          "type": "rule",
          "title": "Held sounds",
          "text": "Amharic often holds a consonant a little longer: አማርኛ is a-ma-rən-nya. Writing doesn't show it, so listen for it — the same letters can be two different words."
        },
        {
          "type": "trace",
          "chars": [
            "ኘ",
            "ኛ",
